// Generic inference reads a discovery-time name->type map, and a `foreach` binding used to be written
// into the SHARED map rather than a copy for the loop body — so after the loop the enclosing local was
// still typed as the ELEMENT there, and a generic call took the loop's type for the outer value's.
//
// The binding cannot shadow the outer name (kama has no shadowing — see tests/xfail/binder_shadow_*), so
// the leak is shown the way it actually reaches a program: the loop's `n` is gone at the call below, and
// `ident(x: m)` must infer `E` from `m`'s own declaration rather than from a binding that has ended.
type value E { public int32 n; public ctor of(int32 n) { this.n = n; } }
fn T ident<T>(T x) { return x; }
fn int32 main() {
    InlineArray<int32>#(2) xs = [3, 4];
    E m = E.of(n: 2);
    int32 r = 0;
    foreach (int32 n in xs) { r = r + n; }   // 7 — the elements
    E k = ident(x: m);                        // T = E, not the loop element's int32
    return r + k.n;                           // 9
}
